lido leading by 5-6 points in a district Trump carried", "Expert ratings still lean Republican, citing the R+7 fundamentals and incumbency", "Pulido has outraised expectations with $3M and faces no outside opposition spending"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point Republican, but the freshest evidence runs the other way: two 2026 polls give Pulido a 5-6 point lead, markets put his win odds near 73%, and he holds a strong fundraising and outside-spending position against an incumbent with no supportive outside help. That combination justifies moving past the Tilt R consensus into a narrow Democratic lean.", "watch": "A late round of independent Republican polling or a shift in the national environment could quickly move this back to tossup territory.", "confidence": "low", "notes": ["Incumbent Monica De La Cruz (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Texas's 15th District pairs a Republican incumbent, Monica De La Cruz, against Democrat Bobby Pulido in South Texas territory that has been drifting toward Democrats even as it remains structurally Republican. Expert raters mostly call the race Lean or Tilt Republican, reflecting the district's seven-point Republican lean and the advantages of incumbency.\n\nThe live evidence, however, favors the challenger. Polls from August and July show Pulido ahead by five to six points, prediction markets give him roughly a 73 percent win probability, and he has raised nearly $3 million with substantial outside support while De La Cruz has drawn only modest outside opposition help. The forecast lands as a narrow Democratic lean with wide uncertainty, since the polling is sparse and the district's fundamentals still favor Republicans.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.4454, "margin": -0.75}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6234, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.6548, "margin": 2.0}]}